    "available_at": "2026-11-06T17:10:14.286690+00:00",
    "body": "Three major packaging suppliers disclosed this week that they received federal grand jury subpoenas in September. International Paper and Smurfit Westrock said the Justice Department investigation in Chicago concerns the pricing and sale of containerboard, the paper used to make corrugated shipping boxes, and related products.\nWhat’s Related\nSmurfit Westrock received its subpoena Sept. 22, and International Paper received its subpoena Sept. 24. Packaging Corporation of America said it received a subpoena from the Justice Department’s Antitrust Division in late September seeking company records. All three companies say they are cooperating. Bloomberg reported that the Justice Department has not formally accused any of the companies or their employees of a crime.\n“We are fully cooperating with the investigation,” Smurfit Westrock said in its quarterly filing. The company said it believes its business practices and those of its subsidiaries comply with U.S. antitrust laws.\nContainerboard prices up by about 20%\nContainerboard prices are about 20% higher than last year so far this year, according to Packaging Dive. The publication reported that International Paper and Smurfit Westrock were among the producers that announced three rounds of price increases this year.\nThe price buyers pay for finished boxes depends on their supplier agreements. Bloomberg reported that some contracts use a North American containerboard price index to help set prices.\nA different lawsuit alleges price coordination\nSuppliers can raise prices independently. A separate civil lawsuit filed in July 2025 alleges that major containerboard producers agreed to raise or maintain prices for containerboard and finished packaging products starting in November 2020. The suit also alleges that producers agreed to limit containerboard production capacity to push prices higher. The defendants dispute the allegations.\nA federal judge denied a request to dismiss the civil case in September, allowing it to proceed. The plaintiff voluntarily dropped Smurfit Westrock plc from the lawsuit on Oct. 17, 2025, and could seek to add it back. Two of its subsidiaries remain defendants.",
    "body_sha256": "541ed05077886add0940c1d1a78aef003e2ae334e526de22e73975ce6b36f247",
